INSERT INTO topic_sections (topic_id, title, content, section_type, order_index) VALUES

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000007',
'Concept Overview: Coordinate Geometry',
E'## What Is Coordinate Geometry?\n\nCoordinate geometry (also called analytic geometry or Cartesian geometry) is the study of geometry using a coordinate system. Every point in a plane can be identified by two numbers: its x-coordinate (horizontal) and y-coordinate (vertical).\n\nIn WASSCE Mathematics, coordinate geometry carries 6-10 marks. It bridges algebra and geometry — algebraic equations describe geometric shapes and vice versa.\n\n### Why It Matters\n\nCoordinate geometry is used in:\n- GPS navigation (locating positions)\n- Computer graphics (placing pixels and shapes on screen)\n- Engineering design (blueprints and CAD software)\n- Physics (mapping motion and trajectories)\n- Economics (plotting graphs and trends)\n\n### The Cartesian Plane\n\nThe Cartesian plane has two perpendicular axes:\n- **x-axis**: horizontal axis (positive to the right, negative to the left)\n- **y-axis**: vertical axis (positive upward, negative downward)\n- **Origin**: The point where axes cross, coordinates (0, 0)\n- **Quadrants**: The four regions formed by the axes\n  - Quadrant I: (+x, +y)\n  - Quadrant II: (-x, +y)\n  - Quadrant III: (-x, -y)\n  - Quadrant IV: (+x, -y)\n\n### WASSCE Key Areas\n\n1. Distance between two points\n2. Midpoint of a line segment\n3. Gradient (slope) of a line\n4. Equations of straight lines (y = mx + c; ax + by + c = 0)\n5. Parallel and perpendicular lines\n6. Finding the equation of a line through given points\n7. Intersection of two lines',
'overview', 1),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000007',
'Definitions & Key Terminologies',
E'## All Essential Terms and Formulas\n\n### Basic Definitions\n\n**Coordinates**: An ordered pair (x, y) giving the position of a point on the Cartesian plane.\n**Abscissa**: The x-coordinate (horizontal position) of a point.\n**Ordinate**: The y-coordinate (vertical position) of a point.\n**Collinear points**: Three or more points that all lie on the same straight line.\n**Locus**: The set of all points satisfying a given condition.\n\n### Distance Formula\n\nThe distance between points A(x1, y1) and B(x2, y2):\n\n**d = sqrt[(x2 - x1)^2 + (y2 - y1)^2]**\n\nThis comes directly from Pythagoras: the horizontal change is (x2-x1), vertical change is (y2-y1), and the distance is the hypotenuse.\n\nExample: Distance from A(1, 2) to B(4, 6):\nd = sqrt[(4-1)^2 + (6-2)^2] = sqrt[9 + 16] = sqrt[25] = 5\n\n### Midpoint Formula\n\nThe midpoint M of segment AB where A(x1, y1) and B(x2, y2):\n\n**M = ((x1 + x2)/2, (y1 + y2)/2)**\n\nExample: Midpoint of A(2, 4) and B(8, 10):\nM = ((2+8)/2, (4+10)/2) = (5, 7)\n\n### Gradient (Slope)\n\nThe gradient measures how steep a line is. It is the ratio of vertical change to horizontal change.\n\n**m = (y2 - y1) / (x2 - x1)**\n\n- Positive gradient: line goes up from left to right\n- Negative gradient: line goes down from left to right\n- Zero gradient: horizontal line\n- Undefined gradient: vertical line\n\nExample: Gradient of line through (1, 3) and (4, 9):\nm = (9-3)/(4-1) = 6/3 = 2\n\n### Equations of a Straight Line\n\n**Slope-Intercept Form**: y = mx + c\n- m = gradient\n- c = y-intercept (where line crosses y-axis)\n\n**Point-Slope Form**: y - y1 = m(x - x1)\n- Used when you know a point (x1, y1) and gradient m\n\n**Two-Point Form**: (y - y1)/(y2 - y1) = (x - x1)/(x2 - x1)\n- Used when you know two points\n\n**General Form**: ax + by + c = 0\n- Can represent any line (including vertical lines)\n\n### Parallel and Perpendicular Lines\n\n**Parallel lines**: Same gradient, different y-intercepts\nm1 = m2\n\n**Perpendicular lines**: Gradients are negative reciprocals\nm1 x m2 = -1\nor: m2 = -1/m1\n\nExample: A line with gradient 3 is parallel to any line with gradient 3.\nA line perpendicular to it has gradient -1/3.',
'definitions', 2),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000007',
'Deep Explanation: Coordinate Geometry Methods',
E'## Complete Method Guides\n\n### Part 1: Using the Distance Formula\n\nAlways subtract in the same order: (second minus first) for both x and y.\n\n**Finding if a triangle is right-angled**: Calculate all three side lengths using the distance formula, then check if a^2 + b^2 = c^2 (Pythagoras).\n\n**Example**: Show that A(0,0), B(3,0), C(0,4) form a right-angled triangle.\nAB = sqrt[(3-0)^2 + (0-0)^2] = sqrt[9] = 3\nAC = sqrt[(0-0)^2 + (4-0)^2] = sqrt[16] = 4\nBC = sqrt[(3-0)^2 + (0-4)^2] = sqrt[9+16] = sqrt[25] = 5\nCheck: 3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2 ✓ Right-angled at A.\n\n### Part 2: Finding the Equation of a Line\n\n**Given gradient m and y-intercept c**: Write y = mx + c directly.\n\n**Given gradient m and one point (x1, y1)**:\n1. Use y - y1 = m(x - x1)\n2. Expand and simplify to y = mx + c\n\nExample: Gradient = 3, passes through (2, 1)\ny - 1 = 3(x - 2)\ny - 1 = 3x - 6\ny = 3x - 5\n\n**Given two points**:\n1. Calculate gradient: m = (y2 - y1)/(x2 - x1)\n2. Use point-slope form with either point\n3. Simplify\n\nExample: Through (1, 2) and (3, 8)\nm = (8-2)/(3-1) = 6/2 = 3\ny - 2 = 3(x - 1)\ny = 3x - 1\n\n### Part 3: Parallel Lines\n\nParallel lines have equal gradients. If you need a line parallel to y = 2x + 5 passing through (3, 1):\nGradient = 2 (same as given line)\ny - 1 = 2(x - 3)\ny = 2x - 5\n\n**Check**: Both lines have gradient 2 and different y-intercepts (+5 and -5), so they are parallel.\n\n### Part 4: Perpendicular Lines\n\nThe product of gradients of perpendicular lines = -1.\n\nIf m1 is the gradient of a line, the perpendicular gradient m2 = -1/m1.\n\nExample: Find the equation of the line perpendicular to y = (1/2)x + 3, passing through (2, 4).\nGradient of given line = 1/2\nPerpendicular gradient = -1/(1/2) = -2\ny - 4 = -2(x - 2)\ny - 4 = -2x + 4\ny = -2x + 8\n\n### Part 5: Finding Intersection Points\n\nTo find where two lines cross, solve them simultaneously.\n\nExample: y = 2x + 1 and y = -x + 7\nSet equal: 2x + 1 = -x + 7\n3x = 6\nx = 2\ny = 2(2) + 1 = 5\nIntersection: (2, 5)\n\n### Part 6: The Gradient of Special Lines\n\n**Horizontal line** y = k: gradient = 0. Example: y = 4 has gradient 0.\n**Vertical line** x = k: gradient is undefined. Example: x = 3 is vertical.\n\n**Key application**: The gradient of a line segment joining the centre of a circle to any point on the circle, multiplied by the gradient of the tangent at that point, equals -1. (Radius is perpendicular to tangent.)\n\n### Part 7: Section Formula (Dividing a Line)\n\nIf point P divides segment AB in ratio m:n, then:\n\nP = ((m*x2 + n*x1)/(m+n), (m*y2 + n*y1)/(m+n))\n\nSpecial case — midpoint: m:n = 1:1, giving the midpoint formula.',
'explanation', 3),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000007',
'Worked Examples: Coordinate Geometry',
E'## Eight Worked Examples (Basic to Exam-Standard)\n\n### Example 1 — Basic: Find the distance between P(-3, 4) and Q(2, -8)\n\nd = sqrt[(x2-x1)^2 + (y2-y1)^2]\n= sqrt[(2-(-3))^2 + (-8-4)^2]\n= sqrt[5^2 + (-12)^2]\n= sqrt[25 + 144]\n= sqrt[169]\n= 13\n\n**Answer: PQ = 13 units**\n\n---\n\n### Example 2 — Basic: Find the midpoint of A(5, -3) and B(-1, 7)\n\nM = ((5 + (-1))/2, (-3 + 7)/2)\n= (4/2, 4/2)\n= (2, 2)\n\n**Answer: Midpoint = (2, 2)**\n\n---\n\n### Example 3 — Intermediate: Find the gradient of the line through (-2, 5) and (4, -1). Hence write its equation.\n\nStep 1: Gradient\nm = (-1 - 5)/(4 - (-2)) = -6/6 = -1\n\nStep 2: Equation using point (-2, 5)\ny - 5 = -1(x - (-2))\ny - 5 = -x - 2\ny = -x + 3\n\n**Answer: m = -1, equation: y = -x + 3**\n\n---\n\n### Example 4 — Intermediate: Determine whether the points A(1, 1), B(3, 5), and C(0, -1) are collinear.\n\nThree points are collinear if the gradient between any two pairs is the same.\n\nGradient AB = (5-1)/(3-1) = 4/2 = 2\nGradient AC = (-1-1)/(0-1) = -2/-1 = 2\n\nBoth gradients are equal, so A, B, C are collinear.\n\n**Answer: The points ARE collinear (all lie on line y = 2x - 1)**\n\n---\n\n### Example 5 — Intermediate: A line L has equation 3x - 2y + 6 = 0. Find (a) its gradient and (b) the equation of the line perpendicular to L passing through (3, 4).\n\n(a) Rearrange to y = mx + c:\n-2y = -3x - 6\ny = (3/2)x + 3\nGradient of L = 3/2\n\n(b) Perpendicular gradient = -1/(3/2) = -2/3\ny - 4 = (-2/3)(x - 3)\ny - 4 = -2x/3 + 2\ny = -2x/3 + 6\nor: 2x + 3y = 18\n\n**Answer: Gradient = 3/2; Perpendicular line: 2x + 3y = 18**\n\n---\n\n### Example 6 — Challenging: The midpoint of AB is M(2, 3). If A = (-1, 5), find the coordinates of B.\n\nUsing midpoint formula:\n((-1 + x)/2, (5 + y)/2) = (2, 3)\n\nFrom x-coordinate: (-1 + x)/2 = 2 → -1 + x = 4 → x = 5\nFrom y-coordinate: (5 + y)/2 = 3 → 5 + y = 6 → y = 1\n\n**Answer: B = (5, 1)**\n\n---\n\n### Example 7 — Challenging: Find the equation of the perpendicular bisector of segment PQ where P = (1, 2) and Q = (5, 8).\n\nStep 1: Midpoint of PQ\nM = ((1+5)/2, (2+8)/2) = (3, 5)\n\nStep 2: Gradient of PQ\nm = (8-2)/(5-1) = 6/4 = 3/2\n\nStep 3: Gradient of perpendicular bisector\nm_perp = -2/3\n\nStep 4: Equation through M(3, 5) with gradient -2/3\ny - 5 = (-2/3)(x - 3)\ny - 5 = -2x/3 + 2\ny = -2x/3 + 7\nor: 2x + 3y = 21\n\n**Answer: 2x + 3y = 21**\n\n---\n\n### Example 8 — Exam Standard: Triangle PQR has vertices P(0, 0), Q(6, 0), R(4, 4). (a) Find the length of each side. (b) Find the equation of the median from P to the midpoint of QR.\n\n(a)\nPQ = sqrt[(6-0)^2 + 0^2] = 6 units\nQR = sqrt[(4-6)^2 + (4-0)^2] = sqrt[4+16] = sqrt[20] = 2sqrt(5) approx 4.47 units\nPR = sqrt[(4-0)^2 + (4-0)^2] = sqrt[16+16] = sqrt[32] = 4sqrt(2) approx 5.66 units\n\n(b) Midpoint of QR:\nM = ((6+4)/2, (0+4)/2) = (5, 2)\n\nGradient of PM (from P(0,0) to M(5,2)):\nm = (2-0)/(5-0) = 2/5\n\nEquation through origin with gradient 2/5:\ny = (2/5)x or 2x - 5y = 0\n\n**Answers: PQ = 6, QR = 2sqrt(5), PR = 4sqrt(2); Median: y = (2/5)x**',
'examples', 4),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000007',
'Common Mistakes, Practice Questions & Solutions',
E'## Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n1. **Subtracting coordinates in different orders**: In (y2-y1)/(x2-x1), always take the SAME point as "second" for both numerator and denominator. Mixed order gives wrong sign for gradient.\n\n2. **Forgetting the square root in the distance formula**: Distance = sqrt[(difference)^2 + (difference)^2]. Students often forget to take the final square root.\n\n3. **Confusing parallel and perpendicular gradients**: Parallel = same gradient. Perpendicular = negative reciprocal (flip and negate). Common error: just negating without flipping.\n\n4. **Not checking if a line is vertical**: A vertical line x = k has undefined gradient. Trying to use y = mx + c for a vertical line will fail.\n\n5. **Using the wrong form of the equation**: When given a point and gradient, use point-slope form y - y1 = m(x - x1). Many students try to substitute into y = mx + c and make algebra errors.\n\n6. **Midpoint vs. section formula confusion**: Midpoint is the specific case where ratio = 1:1. For any other ratio, use the general section formula.\n\n---\n\n## Practice Questions\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice\n\n**Question 1**: The distance between (0, 0) and (5, 12) is:\n(A) 7  (B) 13  (C) 17  (D) 25\n\n**Question 2**: The gradient of the line 4x - 2y + 8 = 0 is:\n(A) -2  (B) 4  (C) 2  (D) -4\n\n**Question 3**: A line parallel to y = 3x - 5 has gradient:\n(A) -3  (B) 1/3  (C) 3  (D) -1/3\n\n**Question 4**: The midpoint of (2, -4) and (8, 6) is:\n(A) (10, 2)  (B) (5, 1)  (C) (3, -1)  (D) (6, 2)\n\n### Section B: Short Answer\n\n**Question 5**: Find the equation of the line through (0, 3) and (4, 11).\n\n**Question 6**: Line L1: y = 2x - 1. Find the equation of line L2 perpendicular to L1 passing through (4, 3).\n\n**Question 7**: Points A(2, 1) and B(k, 7) have gradient 3 between them. Find k.\n\n### Section C: WASSCE-Style\n\n**Question 8**: The coordinates of two points are A(3, -2) and B(-1, 6).\n(a) Find the length AB.\n(b) Find the midpoint of AB.\n(c) Find the equation of the perpendicular bisector of AB.\n\n---\n\n## Full Solutions\n\n**Q1**: B — sqrt[25 + 144] = sqrt[169] = 13\n\n**Q2**: C — Rearrange: -2y = -4x - 8; y = 2x + 4; gradient = 2\n\n**Q3**: C — Same gradient as original line = 3\n\n**Q4**: B — ((2+8)/2, (-4+6)/2) = (5, 1)\n\n**Q5**: m = (11-3)/(4-0) = 8/4 = 2; y-intercept = 3; y = 2x + 3\n\n**Q6**: m(L1) = 2; m(L2) = -1/2\ny - 3 = (-1/2)(x - 4)\ny - 3 = -x/2 + 2\ny = -x/2 + 5 or x + 2y = 10\n\n**Q7**: m = (7-1)/(k-2) = 3\n6 = 3(k-2)\n6 = 3k - 6\n3k = 12\nk = 4\n\n**Q8**:\n(a) AB = sqrt[(-1-3)^2 + (6-(-2))^2] = sqrt[16 + 64] = sqrt[80] = 4sqrt(5) approx 8.94 units\n(b) Midpoint M = ((3+(-1))/2, (-2+6)/2) = (1, 2)\n(c) Gradient AB = (6-(-2))/(-1-3) = 8/(-4) = -2\nPerpendicular gradient = 1/2\ny - 2 = (1/2)(x - 1)\ny = x/2 + 3/2 or x - 2y + 3 = 0',
'practice', 5);
